# S6 — named certificate premise slots worked example (lara-syntax@0.6)
#
# The first worked example authored in the @0.6 symbolic certificate spelling
# (#105): an ord@1 certificate may cite a premise slot by the SOURCE NAME that
# fills it — `(prem base_cell)` instead of `(prem 0)` — and elaboration lowers
# the name to the byte-identical numeric payload the backend already decodes.
# The names below are the same ones the argument's own `from […]` list uses,
# so the certificate reads in the artifact's vocabulary instead of asking the
# reader to count slots.
#
# THE COMMITTED GOLDEN IS THE STANDING BYTE-IDENTITY WITNESS FOR #105. This
# file is the repo's living author of the symbolic spelling, and its
# example.core.sexp was verified byte-equal to the one produced by the numeric
# twin of this artifact (same program, certificate payload written
# `(ordcmp (prem 0) (prem 1))`). Because the golden carries only the LOWERED
# payload, test/WorkedExamplesSpec.hs's freshness property re-proves that
# byte-identity on every CI run: if lowering ever produced anything but the
# numeric spelling's bytes, this example would go stale and the suite would
# fail. test/CertSlotsSpec.hs pins the same property as in-memory fixtures;
# this directory pins it as committed artifact bytes.
#
# The argument is S2's strict leg, hand-authored. S2 writes the same
# comparison as a `comparison` block, whose generated certificate cites its
# slots numerically; S6 writes the `arg` and its certificate by hand, because
# the symbolic spelling is an AUTHORING surface and only an authored
# certificate exercises it. No defeasible bridge: S2 carries the layering
# story, and S6's claim is deliberately the bare arithmetic, so the one
# argument here is the whole unit.
#
# Expected verdict is at the bottom of this file (golden oracle).

artifact ord_named_demo at sha256:5656565656565656565656565656565656565656565656565656565656565656
policy ord-named-v1
use backends [ord@1]

# --- The claim: the bare ordered comparison ----------------------------------------------------

claim c1
  nl      = "The reported baseline accuracy 0.71 is strictly below the reported system accuracy 0.74"
  formal  = num_lt(0.71, 0.74)
  binding = { author = alice, rationale = "Certified ordered comparison of two reported accuracy cells.", audit-status = reviewed }

# --- The two measured cells the certificate consults ------------------------------------------
# One leaf per reported cell, named for the ROLE its cell plays: these names are what the
# certificate below cites. Their truth is observed evidence, never certified content — the
# certificate discharges only that each cited premise's numeric literal equals the corresponding
# goal numeral and that 0.71 < 0.74 (the factivity firewall, as in S2).

leaf base_cell : reports(exp1, score_cell(sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.71))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#row=sys_base]

leaf new_cell : reports(exp1, score_cell(sys_new, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.74))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#row=sys_new]

# --- The argument: one strict step, certificate cited by name ---------------------------------
# The @0.6 point, in one line: the certificate names `base_cell` and `new_cell` — the same
# identifiers the `from […]` list resolves — rather than the 0-based slots they land in.
# Elaboration resolves each name against this argument's own premise list and rewrites it to
# `(prem 0)` / `(prem 1)`; the Unit, the wire encoding, and the ord@1 replay are byte-for-byte
# those of the numeric spelling. A name that resolves to no slot, to two slots, or ambiguously
# is a located elaboration error, never a guess (fail-closed, D2).

arg a1 : supports(c1) by lt_recheck from [base_cell, new_cell]
  assurance = cert(ord@1, sha256:ord-named-v1-theory-0, (ordcmp (prem base_cell) (prem new_cell)))

status c1

# ==============================================================================================
# EXPECTED VERDICT (golden oracle)
# ==============================================================================================
# Compiled AF: a1 (the strict ord@1 comparison step), unattacked -> in (grounded).
# Claim status:
#   c1 -> justified (a1 is a complete argument for it, with no attackers)
